HST Office: every document tab shows the UI mode and the toolbars of its own module.

User report (13/11/2026): Sheets and Writer tabs in one window, the user interface set to "Standard Toolbar"; after a
tab switch the window showed only the menubar and the tab strip, without toolbars or ribbon.
Cause (reproduced on Windows with 1.0.1): .uno:ToolbarMode applies a mode to the frames of SfxViewFrame::Current()'s
module only. "Apply to All" sets the other modules by configuration only. When a tab of such a module is shown,
SfxNotebookBar::StateMethod closes its ribbon, but nothing shows the toolbars of the new mode. The object bars that
SfxWorkWindow requests stay hidden, because an earlier switch to a ribbon mode stored them as invisible
(WindowState Visible=false). Choosing the same mode again for that module did nothing either, because .uno:ToolbarMode
returned early when the mode did not change. The ribbon checks also read the mode of SfxViewFrame::Current()'s module,
which with document tabs can be another tab's module. A document of that module opened from the window (a new tab)
got neither the ribbon nor the toolbars either.
- SfxNotebookBar::IsActiveFor(xFrame): the mode of xFrame's module. StateMethod and SfxWorkWindow (which destroys the
  toolbars under a ribbon) use the frame they work on; IsActive() keeps the Current() behaviour for its other callers.
- StateMethod: when it closes the ribbon of a frame whose module is now in a toolbar mode, it creates and shows that
  mode's "Toolbars" and "UserToolbars", like .uno:ToolbarMode does for its own module (open windows and tabs).
- "Apply to All" (cui UIPickerDialog) also stores, for every other application, the toolbars of the new mode as
  visible and the toolbars only other modes use as invisible (WindowState), so new windows and tabs of those
  applications get the toolbars of their mode and no toolbar of another mode.
- SfxWorkWindow: when its frame becomes visible again (a parked document tab is shown), it updates the object bars.
  An update while the tab was hidden had destroyed its toolbars and status bar, and nothing requested them again:
  a document opened as a new tab lost its toolbars on the first switch back to it. LayoutManager sends VISIBLE
  after its toolbars may be shown again (before, a request at VISIBLE found them still master-hidden and was
  refused).
- StateMethod shows or hides the menubars of xFrame's module (ShowMenubarForModuleOf); before, setting up a tab in
  Tabbed mode could hide the menubar of another module's tabs. A frame still being loaded (no module yet) changes
  nothing. When it closes the ribbon of a frame it also shows that frame's own menubar (a tab parked while its
  module went through ribbon modes came back to Standard Toolbar without a menubar).
- .uno:ToolbarMode no longer returns early when the mode is unchanged, so choosing it again ("Apply to <module>")
  repairs a module whose toolbars were lost.
License: modified upstream files keep their license (MPL-2.0).
Test: tests/unit/test_ui_mode_per_tab.py; GUI: tests/runtime/check-windows-ui-mode-tabs.py (Windows),
tests/runtime/check-ui-mode-tabs.py (Linux).
